---
import Exhibit from "../components/Exhibit.astro";
import ForecastChart from "../components/ForecastChart.astro";
import ChapterOpener from "../components/ChapterOpener.astro";
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import PractitionerNotes from "../components/PractitionerNotes.astro";
import Definitions from "../components/Definitions.astro";
import Pager from "../components/Pager.astro";
import { load, type Frame } from "../lib/data";

const meta = load("meta");
const vol = load<Frame>("vol");
const { y, mu, ewma_log_vol: ewma } = vol.series;
const h: number = meta.horizon;
const firstYear = meta.splits.test.start.slice(0, 4);
const lastYear = meta.splits.test.end.slice(0, 4);

// Typical miss of each forecast on the test period: root mean squared error in log volatility.
function rmse(pred: (i: number) => number | null) {
  let s = 0, n = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < y.length; i++) {
    const p = pred(i), v = y[i];
    if (p != null && v != null) { s += (p - v) ** 2; n++; }
  }
  return Math.sqrt(s / n);
}
// Last week's realized volatility is known on day t: the target that ended h days ago.
const steps = [
  { name: "Last week's volatility", how: "Assume the coming week looks like the one just gone.", e: rmse((i) => (i >= h ? y[i - h] : null)) },
  { name: "Smoothed average (EWMA)", how: "Weight recent days more, older days less: the RiskMetrics recipe.", e: rmse((i) => ewma[i]) },
  { name: "Learned model", how: "A ridge regression on daily, weekly and monthly volatility, EWMA and the VIX.", e: rmse((i) => mu[i]) },
];
const factor = (e: number) => Math.exp(e).toFixed(2);
const best = factor(steps.at(-1)!.e);
const label = `Forecast and realized volatility, ${firstYear}–${lastYear}. The forecast follows the broad level but misses the spikes.`;
---
<section class="chapter" id="forecast" aria-labelledby="forecast-h">
  <ChapterOpener n="3" title="A forecast" minutes={2} question="How do you predict next week's volatility?" hid="forecast-h" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Volatility is the easiest thing in finance to forecast, because it clusters: a bumpy week is
      usually followed by another. I built up the forecast in three steps, each judged by its typical
      miss on the test period.
    </p>
  </div>

  <table class="ladder">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">Typical forecast miss for three methods</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col" class="num">Typical miss</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        steps.map((s, i) => (
          <tr class={i === steps.length - 1 ? "focus" : ""}>
            <th scope="row">{s.name}<span class="how">{s.how}</span></th>
            <td class="num">×{factor(s.e)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="table-note">
    “×{best}” means the forecast is typically off by a factor of {best} in either direction: a
    forecast of 10% usually lands between about {(10 / +best).toFixed(1)}% and {(10 * +best).toFixed(0)}%.
    Test period, {firstYear}–{lastYear}.
  </p>

  <div class="exhibit-wide">
    <Exhibit
      letter="D"
      title="The learned forecast against what happened"
      source={`Forecast (black) and realized (grey) 5-day portfolio volatility, annualized, log scale, ${firstYear}–${lastYear}. Each forecast was made with data up to that day only.`}
      id="exhibit-d"
    >
      <ForecastChart frame={vol} label={label} />
      <ForecastChart frame={vol} label={label} narrow />
    </Exhibit>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The learned model is the best of the three, but look at the spikes: the forecast is a smooth
      line through a jagged world. Any single number will be wrong, often by a lot. The useful question
      is not “how good is the forecast?” but “how wrong could it be, and can I say so honestly?”
      That is what the next chapter adds.
    </p>
  </div>

  <PractitionerNotes>
    <p>
      Target: log of realized daily volatility over t+1…t+{h}, √(mean r²). Features at the close of t:
      HAR components (1, 5 and 22-day realized volatility), EWMA portfolio and asset volatilities
      (λ = 0.94), and the VIX level and 5-day change. Ridge regression, fitted once on the training
      period (out-of-fold RMSE {meta.model.oof_rmse.toFixed(3)}). Typical miss is exp(RMSE) in log volatility;
      RMSE values: {steps.map((s) => s.e.toFixed(3)).join(", ")}. A HAR model and LightGBM were also
      tried; ridge was best out of sample.
    </p>
  </PractitionerNotes>

  <Definitions
    items={[
      { id: "ewma", term: "EWMA", def: "Exponentially weighted moving average: recent days count more. Used by RiskMetrics since 1994." },
    ]}
  />
  <Pager id="forecast" />
</section>

<style>
  .ladder {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: var(--s1) 0 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .ladder thead th {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-variant-caps: all-small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    padding-bottom: var(--s-1);
  }
  .ladder tbody th,
  .ladder td {
    padding: var(--s0) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    vertical-align: top;
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .ladder .num {
    text-align: right;
    white-space: nowrap;
    padding-left: var(--s1);
  }
  .how {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .focus th,
  .focus td {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .table-note {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--s0);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
</style>
